add("pole-attract", "magnetism", "induction", [7], "How do unlike magnetic poles interact?", ["They attract", "They repel", "They always become electrically neutral wires", "They must stop all flux outside the magnet"], "North and south are unlike poles and attract one another in the lesson. This complements the like-pole repulsion rule; reversing one magnet changes which pole relationship is present.", ["Unlike magnetic poles _____.", "attract"]);
add("flux", "magnetism", "induction", [6], "What are the lesson's magnetic lines of force also called?", ["Lines of magnetic flux", "Lines of electrical resistance", "Lines of electrolyte density", "Lines of mechanical torque only"], "Flux lines represent the magnetic field pattern. They are a way of describing the field, not physical wires and not the resistive path of a separate electrical circuit.", ["The magnetic lines of force are also called lines of _____.", "flux"]);
add("permeability", "magnetism", "induction", [11], "What is magnetic permeability?", ["Ease with which magnetic flux is established in a material or magnetic path", "Opposition to establishing magnetic flux", "Electrical resistance of an open wire", "Only magnetization left after a field is removed"], "Permeability describes how readily the magnetic path supports flux. Reluctance describes opposition, while residual magnetism describes magnetization that remains without the external influence.", ["Ease of establishing magnetic flux is called _____.", "permeability"]);
add("reluctance", "magnetism", "induction", [12], "What is magnetic reluctance?", ["Opposition to establishing magnetic flux", "Ease with which a material supports magnetic flux", "The number of waveform cycles per second", "The amount of electrolyte in a cell"], "The lesson compares reluctance with resistance as an opposition concept, while permeability describes ease of flux establishment. They are related distinctions, not alternate names for the same property.", ["Opposition to magnetic flux is called _____.", "reluctance"]);
add("iron-permeability", "magnetism", "induction", [9, 11, 12], "Which magnetic description fits the iron-core example used in the lesson?", ["High permeability", "Very high reluctance to useful flux", "No response to a magnetic field", "Only electrical capacitance, with no magnetic property"], "The lesson uses iron as a material in which useful magnetic flux is readily established. That supports high permeability and relatively low reluctance for this comparison, not the quiz's proposed high-reluctance claim.", ["Iron's ease of supporting flux is described as high _____.", "permeability"]);
add("iron-reluctance", "magnetism", "induction", [11, 12, 29], "Compared with a reluctant magnetic path, what does the lesson's iron core provide?", ["A lower-reluctance path for flux", "A higher-reluctance gap that prevents useful flux", "An electrolyte path between two battery plates", "A nonmagnetic path equivalent to removing the core"], "The iron core concentrates the flux from the coil. Describing it as a lower-reluctance path is consistent with the lesson's high-permeability example and distinguishes it from an opposing or interrupted path.", ["The iron-core example supports flux with relatively _____ reluctance.", "low", ["lower"]]);
add("hysteresis", "magnetism", "induction", [14, 15, 16], "Which graph describes how a magnetic material responds as the magnetizing influence is cycled?", ["Hysteresis loop", "Battery discharge-capacity table", "Commutator wiring diagram", "Specific-gravity temperature table"], "The lesson uses a hysteresis loop to describe a material's magnetization response. It also identifies residual magnetism on that response, connecting the graph with magnetization that remains after the applied influence is removed.", ["The magnetic response graph is a _____ loop.", "hysteresis"]);
add("residual", "magnetism", "induction", [15, 17], "What is residual magnetism?", ["Magnetization remaining after the external magnetizing influence is removed", "Only the current flowing while a charger is connected", "Only the air gap between two armature poles", "Complete absence of magnetization in every part"], "Residual magnetism remains without the external influence that established it. The lesson links this retained magnetization with generator behaviour rather than treating it as current that must still be flowing.", ["Magnetization left without the external influence is _____ magnetism.", "residual", ["remanent"]]);
add("wire-field", "electromagnets", "induction", [19], "What is produced around a conductor carrying electrical current?", ["A magnetic field", "A permanent salt bridge", "Only an electrolyte density change", "A mechanically rotating commutator in every conductor"], "Current through a conductor creates a surrounding magnetic field. The field exists even when the wire itself is not a moving armature; its strength and direction depend on the current arrangement.", ["A current-carrying conductor creates a magnetic _____.", "field"]);
add("current-field", "electromagnets", "induction", [19, 25], "For the same conductor arrangement, what does increasing current do to the magnetic field in the lesson?", ["Increases field strength", "Always reverses the poles without changing current direction", "Eliminates all magnetic flux", "Makes field strength independent of current"], "The current-carrying conductor example states that greater current produces a greater field. Reversal is a separate direction change and should not be confused with increasing the magnitude of the same-direction current.", ["With the arrangement unchanged, more current makes the magnetic field _____.", "stronger"]);
add("straight-rule", "electromagnets", "induction", [21], "For the lesson's straight-conductor left-hand rule, what do the curled fingers indicate when the thumb follows electron flow?", ["Direction of the magnetic field around the conductor", "The battery's state of charge", "The direction of a generator's mechanical rotation in every case", "The electrolyte flow through a separator"], "The source explicitly uses electron flow from negative to positive for this straight-wire rule. The thumb follows that flow and the curled fingers show the surrounding field direction; this is not the separate coil or generator hand rule.", ["In the straight-wire electron-flow rule, curled fingers show the magnetic _____ direction.", "field"]);
add("field-reversal", "electromagnets", "induction", [25, 30], "What happens to the electromagnet's polarity if current direction is reversed?", ["Its polarity reverses", "Its polarity must remain unchanged", "It becomes a primary chemical cell", "Its iron core must permanently lose all permeability"], "The source says that reversing current reverses the coil/core polarity. Changing direction is different from merely changing field strength while current continues in the same direction.", ["Reversing coil current _____ the electromagnet's polarity.", "reverses"]);
add("coil", "electromagnets", "induction", [26, 27, 28], "What is the lesson's name for a conductor wound into many loops?", ["Coil", "Salt bridge", "Commutator bar", "Specific-gravity float"], "The many loops form a coil whose individual fields contribute to the combined field. This gives a useful magnetic arrangement rather than treating each turn as an unrelated isolated wire.", ["Many loops of a conductor form a _____.", "coil"]);
add("iron-core", "electromagnets", "induction", [29, 30], "Why does the lesson place an iron core inside the coil?", ["To concentrate the magnetic flux", "To serve as a lead-acid electrolyte", "To remove every line of flux from the coil", "To change the coil into a split-ring commutator"], "The iron core supports and concentrates the coil's flux and becomes magnetized while current flows. It is not an electrical load that replaces the wire or the source powering the coil.", ["The coil's iron core helps concentrate magnetic _____.", "flux"]);
add("electromagnet", "electromagnets", "induction", [28, 29, 30], "What does a current-carrying coil around an iron core form?", ["Electromagnet", "Permanent-magnet-only assembly with no current dependence", "Chemical storage cell", "Hydrometer"], "The lesson builds the electromagnet by combining the fields of many turns with an iron core. Its field can be controlled through current, distinguishing the energized arrangement from relying only on a permanent magnet.", ["An energized coil with an iron core forms an _____.", "electromagnet", ["electro-magnet"]]);
add("solenoid", "magnetic-devices", "induction", [35, 36, 37, 38], "Which device uses an energized coil to pull a movable iron core inward?", ["Solenoid", "Hydrometer", "Permanent salt bridge", "Commutator"], "The solenoid converts an electrical coil command into movement of its iron core. The source shows the core drawn into the coil when energized, with a spring normally returning the illustrated unit to its off position.", ["The coil-and-moving-core device is a _____.", "solenoid"]);
add("solenoid-off", "magnetic-devices", "induction", [37, 38], "What holds the illustrated solenoid core in its normal off position?", ["Its return spring", "A permanently energized armature winding", "Electrolyte density", "A split-ring rectifier"], "The lesson's illustrated solenoid is spring loaded toward the off position. Energizing the coil pulls the core inward against the spring, so the rest state and the energized state must be distinguished.", ["The illustrated solenoid normally returns under _____ force.", "spring"]);
add("relay", "magnetic-devices", "induction", [35, 39, 40], "What is the useful role of the lesson's relay in controlling aircraft electrical power?", ["An energized coil operates contacts controlling another circuit", "A float directly measures battery electrolyte density", "A chemical reaction stores current in a salt bridge", "A commutator changes AC frequency by mechanical gearing"], "A relay uses a coil-operated contact mechanism to switch another electrical path. This permits the control action to be distinguished from the high-current path being switched; the magnetic core is not the same moving plunger arrangement as the solenoid example.", ["The coil-operated switching device is a _____.", "relay"]);
add("diagram-rest", "magnetic-devices", "induction", [37, 39], "How should the lesson's relay/solenoid diagrams be interpreted unless an energized state is explicitly identified?", ["In their de-energized condition", "As if every coil is continuously energized", "As if every normally open contact is already closed", "As if contact position is unrelated to coil state"], "The source establishes de-energized drawings as its normal convention. Energized examples are explicitly labelled, so the convention must not be stretched into a claim that no electrical diagram can ever show an operating state.", ["Unless a different state is identified, the lesson shows relay diagrams _____.", "de-energized", ["deenergized", "de-energised", "deenergised"]]);
add("relative-motion", "induction", "induction", [24], "Which motion relationship can produce induction in the lesson?", ["Relative motion between the conductor and magnetic field", "Only motion of the conductor with every magnet fixed", "Only motion of the magnet with every conductor fixed", "No relative change between the conductor and field"], "The lesson states that either the conductor or the field can move. What matters is their relative interaction, rather than a rule that the moving part must always be one particular component.", ["The important motion between conductor and field is _____ motion.", "relative"]);
add("self-induction", "induction", "induction", [42, 43], "What describes self-induction in the coil example?", ["A changing current changes its field and induces an EMF in that same circuit", "A steady current with no field change necessarily produces a new induced voltage", "Only a chemical reaction inside a primary cell", "A permanent electrical connection between two isolated coils"], "Building or collapsing coil flux produces an induced voltage in the same circuit. The slide loosely calls the voltage inductance; the question distinguishes the induced EMF from the coil property called inductance.", ["Induced EMF in the circuit whose own current is changing is _____-induction.", "self"]);
add("turns", "induction", "induction", [43, 44], "Which change is listed as increasing the induced effect in the coil construction discussion?", ["Increasing the number of turns", "Removing every turn from the coil", "Keeping the field permanently unchanged with no relative motion", "Replacing the magnetic core with electrolyte"], "The lesson lists number of turns among the factors controlling the coil's inductive behaviour and says that adding conductors can increase induced voltage. The comparison assumes the other relevant conditions are unchanged.", ["The coil's construction includes its number of _____.", "turns", ["windings"]]);
add("form-factor", "induction", "induction", [45], "Which geometrical factor is listed for an inductor?", ["Form factor", "Electrolyte specific gravity", "Battery charge quarter", "Brush material only"], "The inductor discussion names form factor alongside number of turns and core permeability. It refers to construction geometry rather than the chemical or generator-contact properties in the other options.", ["The inductor's geometry is represented by its _____ factor.", "form"]);
add("core-permeability", "induction", "induction", [46], "Which core property does the lesson list as affecting an inductor?", ["Permeability", "Electrolyte acidity", "Commutator segment count alone", "Battery discharge time alone"], "The inductor's core permeability affects how readily its magnetic path supports flux. It belongs with the lesson's turns and form-factor discussion, rather than battery servicing or brush-contact characteristics.", ["The listed magnetic core property affecting an inductor is _____.", "permeability"]);
add("mutual-induction", "induction", "induction", [47], "What is mutual induction?", ["Changing magnetic flux from one coil induces voltage in another without a direct electrical connection", "A salt bridge connecting two chemical half-cells", "A direct metallic short between both coil terminals", "A permanent voltage created by two motionless unchanging fields"], "The lesson places a second coil near a changing magnetic field and induces voltage without connecting the two circuits electrically. Magnetic coupling, not a hidden wire connection, links the circuits.", ["Induction from one coil into another is _____ induction.", "mutual"]);
add("coil-coupling", "induction", "induction", [48], "What does moving the two inductors closer do in the lesson's mutual-induction example?", ["Allows more flux linkage between them", "Necessarily turns both into chemical cells", "Removes the need for a changing field", "Guarantees zero voltage in the second coil"], "The source links closer positioning with more of one coil's changing flux reaching the other. This is a coupling comparison in that example, not a claim that proximity alone replaces the field change needed for induction.", ["More field passing through the second coil means more magnetic _____ linkage.", "flux"]);
add("emi", "interference", "induction", [53, 55, 58], "What does EMI stand for?", ["Electromagnetic interference", "Electrical mechanical indication", "Electronic magnetic insulation", "Electrolyte measurement indication"], "EMI is unwanted electromagnetic energy affecting another circuit or system. The source describes erroneous readings or damage in sensitive systems, so the concern is coupling into unintended paths rather than useful generator induction alone.", ["EMI means electromagnetic _____.", "interference"]);
add("rfi", "interference", "induction", [55, 60], "What does RFI stand for?", ["Radio frequency interference", "Regulated field induction", "Rotor frequency indication", "Reverse field insulation"], "RFI concerns interference at radio frequencies and may cause radio disturbances or false indications. It shares suppression principles with EMI, but its name identifies the radio-frequency part of the problem.", ["RFI means radio frequency _____.", "interference"]);
add("shield", "interference", "induction", [56], "What does the metallic braid around a shielded wire do in the lesson?", ["Intercepts unwanted radiated energy and provides its designed ground path", "Replaces every conductor's electrical insulation", "Converts DC into AC using commutator segments", "Makes the wire independent of routing and installation requirements"], "The shield intercepts unwanted energy so it is handled by the designed shielding/bonding arrangement. The number of grounded ends is installation-specific; the slide's single-end wording is not graded as a universal wiring rule.", ["The metallic braid intercepting unwanted radiated energy is a _____.", "shield"]);
add("swpm", "interference", "induction", [58], "Where does the lesson direct the technician to find wiring-bundle separation requirements?", ["Standard Wiring Practices Manual", "Battery capacity chart alone", "A generator's armature nameplate alone", "A general electrolyte neutralizer label"], "The source directs bundle separation questions to the SWPM. Routing and shielding requirements belong to the applicable approved wiring data rather than an assumed universal distance taken from a classroom example.", ["Complete SWPM: Standard Wiring Practices _____.", "Manual"]);
add("flux-valve", "interference", "induction", [59], "What device does the lesson identify for locating compass magnetic sensing away from major interference sources?", ["Flux valve", "Current-limiting relay", "Battery hydrometer", "Generator commutator"], "The lesson identifies a flux valve in the remote compass-sensing arrangement. It is not a general device that erases every interference field; location and the aircraft's compass installation remain part of the system design.", ["The remote magnetic sensing device named in the compass example is a _____ valve.", "flux"]);

add("induced-emf", "generation", "generator", [7, 8], "What is induced when a conductor cuts magnetic lines of force?", ["Electromotive force (EMF)", "Magnetic reluctance as the generated electrical output", "Electrolyte specific gravity", "A permanent salt bridge"], "The generated electrical potential is an EMF, meaning electromotive force. The supplied quiz incorrectly spells this out as electromagnetic force; the generator slides describe induced voltage instead.", ["The generated voltage is an induced _____ (use the abbreviation).", "EMF"]);
add("emf-polarity", "generation", "generator", [7, 10], "With the field direction fixed, what can reverse the polarity of the induced EMF?", ["Reversing the conductor's motion through the field", "Increasing speed without reversing motion", "Adding more turns without reversing their connection", "Increasing field strength without reversing its direction"], "The source relates polarity to the direction in which the conductor cuts the field. Speed, turns and strength affect magnitude in the stated comparisons; a direction reversal changes the polarity relationship.", ["With the field fixed, induced polarity reverses when the direction of conductor _____ reverses.", "motion", ["movement", "rotation"]]);
add("emf-conductors", "generation", "generator", [9, 28], "With speed and field strength unchanged, which change increases the generator's induced EMF in the lesson?", ["More active conductors/turns cutting the field", "Fewer active conductors cutting the same field", "Changing only the battery electrolyte level", "Changing only the armature's paint finish"], "The lesson lists active wires or turns among the three magnitude factors. This separates the conductors cutting flux from a cosmetic change or an unrelated battery measurement.", ["One EMF factor is the number of active _____ cutting the field.", "conductors", ["wires", "turns"]]);
add("emf-field", "generation", "generator", [9, 28], "With speed and active conductors unchanged, what raises induced EMF?", ["A stronger magnetic field", "A weaker magnetic field", "A direction change alone with no magnitude change", "Removal of the magnetic field"], "Magnetic-field strength is one of the lesson's three EMF magnitude factors. Reversing field direction is a polarity issue, while increasing its strength changes the magnitude for the same mechanical arrangement.", ["The three EMF factors include magnetic-field _____.", "strength"]);
add("emf-speed", "generation", "generator", [9, 28], "With active conductors and field strength unchanged, what raises induced EMF?", ["Higher rotation speed", "Lower rotation speed", "Stopping all relative motion", "Only changing rotation direction at the same speed"], "A greater rate of cutting flux increases the induced effect in the lesson's generator comparison. Rotation direction sets polarity; rotation speed is the magnitude factor being asked for here.", ["The three EMF factors include rotational _____.", "speed"]);
add("ac-slip-rings", "generation", "generator", [12, 29], "What current-collection arrangement identifies the lesson's simple AC generator?", ["Two slip rings and brushes", "A split-ring commutator and brushes", "A battery hydrometer and float", "A salt bridge and two chemical electrodes"], "The lesson takes the alternating output from the rotating loop through slip rings and brushes. The DC example instead uses a split-ring commutator to change the external connection during rotation.", ["The simple AC generator uses slip _____.", "rings", ["ring"]]);
add("dc-commutation", "generation", "generator", [14, 15, 30], "What makes the external output unidirectional in the lesson's DC generator?", ["The commutator and brushes mechanically rectify the armature output", "Slip rings leave both coil ends continuously connected to the same rings", "A salt bridge absorbs the reverse half-cycle", "The armature produces no changing polarity internally"], "The rotating armature generates alternating EMF, but commutator switching presents unidirectional output at the brushes. DC output does not mean that the underlying rotating coil never experiences polarity reversal.", ["The split-ring device mechanically rectifying armature output is the _____.", "commutator"]);
add("sine-wave", "waveforms", "generator", [13], "Which current is represented by the lesson's sine-wave signal alternating between positive and negative?", ["Alternating current", "Steady direct current", "Electrolyte current capacity", "Magnetic reluctance"], "The changing sign in the plotted waveform identifies alternating current. An oscilloscope displays a signal versus time; the probe and setup determine whether that signal represents voltage, current or another measured quantity.", ["The waveform changing between positive and negative represents _____ current.", "alternating", ["AC"]]);
add("cycle", "waveforms", "generator", [17], "What is one complete waveform cycle?", ["A complete pattern returning to its starting state", "Only the positive peak", "Only one instantaneous sample", "Only the difference between two unequal peaks"], "A cycle includes the complete sequence of waveform changes before the pattern repeats. It is not just one peak or one reading taken during the sequence, and frequency counts how often these complete cycles occur.", ["One complete repeating waveform pattern is a _____.", "cycle"]);
add("frequency", "waveforms", "generator", [18], "What is the number of AC cycles occurring per second called?", ["Frequency", "Peak voltage", "Instantaneous voltage", "Phase difference"], "Frequency counts complete cycles per second and is expressed in hertz. Peak and instantaneous values describe magnitude, while phase describes relative timing rather than cycle count.", ["The number of cycles per second is called _____.", "frequency"]);
add("hertz", "waveforms", "generator", [18], "Which unit expresses AC frequency?", ["Hertz", "Volts", "Amperes", "Ampere-hours"], "Hertz is the unit for cycles per second. Volts and amperes measure electrical magnitude, and ampere-hours describe battery capacity over time rather than waveform repetition rate.", ["The unit for cycles per second is _____.", "hertz", ["Hz"]]);
add("in-phase", "waveforms", "generator", [19], "When are the voltage and current waveforms described as in phase in the lesson?", ["Their corresponding peaks occur at the same time", "Their corresponding peaks occur at different times", "Their amplitudes must both be zero at all times", "They must have different frequencies"], "The lesson's in-phase example aligns corresponding peaks in time. Matching timing is the distinction; it does not require the voltage and current to have the same numerical magnitude.", ["Waveforms with corresponding peaks at the same time are in _____.", "phase"]);
add("out-phase", "waveforms", "generator", [20], "What distinguishes the lesson's out-of-phase waveforms?", ["Corresponding points occur at different times", "They have identical timing at every point", "They must represent only battery capacity", "Their peaks cannot be measured"], "Out-of-phase waveforms differ in relative timing, so corresponding maxima and minima do not align as in the in-phase example. This is a timing distinction rather than a definition of voltage size.", ["A relative timing shift between waveforms is a difference in _____.", "phase"]);
add("instantaneous", "waveforms", "generator", [21], "What is the value of voltage or current at a particular point in a cycle called?", ["Instantaneous value", "Frequency", "Peak-to-peak value regardless of sampling time", "Battery ampere-hour capacity"], "An instantaneous value belongs to one specified point in the cycle. It may be smaller than the peak and is not itself a count of cycles or a battery storage rating.", ["A reading at one point in the cycle is an _____ value.", "instantaneous"]);
add("peak", "waveforms", "generator", [22], "What is the largest instantaneous magnitude reached within the lesson's cycle?", ["Peak value", "Frequency", "Electrolyte density", "Number of series cells"], "The peak is the cycle's largest instantaneous voltage or current magnitude. This is different from the effective/RMS value used to compare the heating effect of AC and DC.", ["The maximum instantaneous waveform value is its _____ value.", "peak"]);
add("rms", "waveforms", "generator", [23, 24], "What does the lesson's effective/RMS AC value represent in its resistive-load comparison?", ["The DC value producing the equivalent heating effect", "Always the waveform's positive peak", "The number of AC cycles per second", "The number of armature conductors"], "The lesson compares AC RMS with a DC value producing the same heating in a resistor. RMS is not the same definition as peak or frequency, and no peak/RMS calculation is included in this bank.", ["Complete RMS: root mean _____.", "square"]);
add("ripple", "generation", "generator", [26, 27, 28], "How does the lesson make DC generator output smoother?", ["Use more appropriately arranged armature coils", "Remove all but one active coil", "Replace the commutator with two slip rings", "Make the field vanish between each brush contact"], "The lesson adds coils so their rectified contributions overlap and reduce the deep gaps in the output. Smoother DC therefore comes from the armature arrangement, not from pretending a single rectified loop has perfectly constant output.", ["The lesson reduces DC ripple by adding more armature _____.", "coils", ["windings"]]);
add("field-frame", "generator-parts", "generator", [32], "What are the field frame's two roles in the generator lesson?", ["Complete the magnetic path and support other components", "Rectify output and measure electrolyte density", "Provide only a rotating commutator contact surface", "Store chemical charge and replace the field coils"], "The field frame provides the magnetic return path between the poles and mechanical support. Current collection and armature rotation are separate component functions rather than alternate names for the frame.", ["The supporting part completing the magnetic path is the field _____.", "frame"]);
add("laminations", "generator-parts", "generator", [33], "Why does the lesson use laminated magnetic poles?", ["To reduce eddy-current losses", "To remove all magnetic permeability", "To make the poles into battery separators", "To replace brush contact with electrolyte"], "The source connects lamination with reducing eddy-current losses in the magnetic material. The magnetic component still has to carry useful flux; lamination is not intended to make it magnetically inactive.", ["The source reduces eddy-current losses by using _____ poles.", "laminated"]);
add("field-windings", "generator-parts", "generator", [34, 35], "What supplies the controllable main magnetic field in the lesson's wound-field generator?", ["Energized field windings", "The battery's electrolyte alone", "Only the commutator insulation", "Only a disconnected brush holder"], "The field windings act as electromagnets when energized. This lets the system control field strength electrically, unlike relying solely on an unchanging permanent-magnet field.", ["The stationary main-field coils are called field _____.", "windings", ["coils"]]);
add("armature", "generator-parts", "generator", [36, 38], "Which assembly rotates through the field in the lesson's DC generator?", ["Armature", "Stationary field frame", "Battery separator", "Fixed regulator relay case"], "The armature is mounted on the rotating shaft and carries its conductors through the magnetic field. The stationary field structure provides the field while the armature supplies the relative motion and induced output.", ["The rotating generator assembly carrying its coils is the _____.", "armature"]);
add("windings", "generator-parts", "generator", [37], "What are the loops of wire evenly spaced around the armature called?", ["Windings", "Brushes", "Mica segments", "Pole shoes"], "The wire loops are the armature windings. Brushes collect current at the commutator, while insulation and poles have different roles, so those parts should not be confused with the active wire loops.", ["The armature's loops of wire are called _____.", "windings", ["coils"]]);
add("ring-armature", "generator-parts", "generator", [39], "What limitation is described for the Gramme-ring armature?", ["Its inner windings cut fewer useful flux lines", "It has no windings around a core", "It is the same slotted drum construction under another name", "It eliminates the need for a current-collection arrangement"], "The lesson says the ring's inner windings cut fewer lines of flux and describes limited aircraft use. This contrasts with the drum arrangement, where the winding surrounds the slotted iron core.", ["The ring construction named in the lesson is the _____-ring armature.", "Gramme", ["gramme-ring"]]);
add("drum-armature", "generator-parts", "generator", [39], "Which construction identifies the drum-type armature shown on the right of the review comparison?", ["Windings arranged in slots and surrounding a drum-shaped iron core", "Windings arranged only around the lesson's annular Gramme ring", "A stationary field frame without a rotating core", "Only a detached slip ring with no armature winding"], "The right-hand picture shows the drum's slotted iron core with windings around it. The left-hand picture is the ring example; the source labels individual drum components without labelling it as a different chemical or magnetic device.", ["A slotted iron core surrounded by windings identifies the _____ armature.", "drum"]);
add("lap", "generator-parts", "generator", [40], "Which armature winding arrangement does the lesson associate with low-voltage, high-current applications?", ["Lap winding", "Wave winding", "A salt-bridge connection", "Only an unwound permanent-magnet rotor"], "The lap example has parallel armature paths and is associated with high current and lower voltage. The wave example is the lesson's higher-voltage, lower-current comparison.", ["The lesson's high-current armature winding is the _____ type.", "lap"]);
add("wave", "generator-parts", "generator", [40], "Which armature winding arrangement does the lesson associate with higher voltage and lower current?", ["Wave winding", "Lap winding", "Only a direct short across the brushes", "Only a primary-cell series chain"], "The wave description emphasizes series-connected contributions and additive voltage. It contrasts with the lap arrangement's parallel paths and high-current application rather than being a synonym for lap winding.", ["The lesson's higher-voltage armature winding is the _____ type.", "wave"]);
add("commutator", "generator-parts", "generator", [42], "Which component is made of wedge-shaped copper segments separated by mica at the armature end?", ["Commutator", "Slip ring", "Carbon brush", "Stationary pole shoe"], "The segmented copper and mica construction identifies the commutator. Segments provide the changing electrical connection during rotation, while mica insulates adjacent segments; brushes ride on this assembly rather than forming its copper bars.", ["The copper-segment assembly insulated by mica is the _____.", "commutator"]);
add("commutator-photo", "generator-parts", "generator", [36, 42], "Which component is indicated by the upper orange arrow pointing to the segmented copper cylinder in the photograph?", ["Commutator", "Armature windings", "Drive shaft", "Cooling fan"], "The upper arrow points to the segmented cylinder at the armature's end. The lower arrows indicate other construction details; identifying the segmented contact surface distinguishes the commutator from the shaft, wire coils and fan.", ["The upper arrow points to the segmented copper _____.", "commutator"]);
add("mica", "generator-parts", "generator", [42], "What separates adjacent commutator copper segments electrically?", ["Mica", "Spongy lead", "Battery electrolyte", "Conductive carbon paste"], "Mica is the thin insulating material between segments in the source's construction. Electrical separation matters because the commutator must connect the intended coil paths rather than shorting all segments together.", ["The commutator's thin segment insulation is _____.", "mica"]);
add("brushes", "generator-parts", "generator", [43], "What material does the lesson specify for generator brushes?", ["High-grade carbon", "Mica", "Spongy lead", "Cadmium hydroxide"], "The lesson specifies carbon for the brushes that contact the rotating commutator. Mica belongs to segment insulation, so it would not replace the current-collecting brush material.", ["The generator brushes are made of high-grade _____.", "carbon"]);
add("armature-reaction", "armature-reaction", "generator", [45, 46, 48], "What does armature reaction do in the loaded generator example?", ["Distorts the main field through the armature current's magnetic field", "Changes electrolyte density in the battery", "Makes the main field independent of every armature current", "Eliminates all magnetic interaction between armature and field"], "Under load, armature current creates a magnetic field that distorts the main field. The lesson associates that shifted magnetic condition with commutation problems and sparking, motivating corrective arrangements.", ["Distortion of the main field by armature current is armature _____.", "reaction"]);
add("sparking", "armature-reaction", "generator", [46, 49], "What damage is associated with poor commutation and sparking in the lesson?", ["Pitting of the commutator surface", "A higher hydrometer float in the battery", "Elimination of all carbon-brush wear", "Automatic restoration of every winding's insulation"], "Sparking at the rotating contact surface can pit the commutator rather than improving contact. The course discusses brush position and corrective magnetic arrangements because contact condition and commutation matter.", ["Sparking can cause commutator surface _____.", "pitting"]);
add("brush-position", "armature-reaction", "generator", [50, 58], "What governs adjustment of generator brush position in the lesson's maintenance context?", ["The specified manufacturer setting/procedure", "Any position that produces visible sparking", "Only the colour of the generator case", "Only the battery's electrolyte level"], "The slides describe brush adjustment or manufacturer setting in relation to the distorted field. Actual adjustment is generator-specific and must not be improvised from the classroom sketch.", ["Brush-setting work follows the generator _____ instructions.", "manufacturer", ["manufacturers", "manufacturer's"]]);
add("compensating", "armature-reaction", "generator", [51], "How are compensating windings connected in the supplied lesson?", ["In series with the armature", "Only across the battery's service caps", "As an isolated chemical salt bridge", "Only in parallel with the hydrometer"], "The source connects compensating windings in series with the armature so their magnetic effect responds to armature current. Their purpose is to counter the armature field and help keep the neutral plane stationary.", ["Compensating windings are connected in _____ with the armature.", "series"]);
add("compensation-role", "armature-reaction", "generator", [51], "What is the stated purpose of compensating windings?", ["Counteract armature-field effects and stabilize the neutral plane", "Store charge chemically between positive and negative plates", "Replace every brush with a slip ring", "Increase commutator sparking as load rises"], "The compensating field opposes the unwanted armature-field effect. The lesson links this with a stable neutral plane rather than requiring repeated brush adjustment for the changing field distortion.", ["The windings opposing armature-field distortion are _____ windings.", "compensating", ["compensation"]]);
add("interpoles", "armature-reaction", "generator", [52], "Where does the lesson place interpoles?", ["Between the main poles", "Inside the battery electrolyte", "Only on the outside of the brush cable insulation", "In place of every armature winding"], "Interpoles are additional magnetic poles between the main poles, introduced in the lesson's armature-reaction solutions. Their placement is part of a corrective magnetic arrangement, not a battery or wiring accessory.", ["The secondary poles between the generator's main poles are _____.", "interpoles", ["interpole"]]);
add("series-field", "field-connections", "generator", [53], "How is the field winding connected in a series-wound DC generator?", ["In series with the load", "In parallel with the load", "Disconnected from every electrical path", "Only through the mechanical shaft"], "The series field carries current in the series load path. This is a connection distinction from shunt winding, where the field is connected across the circuit rather than in the same series path.", ["A series-wound generator's field is connected in _____ with the load.", "series"]);
add("shunt-field", "field-connections", "generator", [54], "How is the field winding connected in a shunt-wound DC generator?", ["In parallel with the load", "In series with the load", "Only through a battery vent cap", "Disconnected whenever the armature rotates"], "Shunt means a parallel field connection in the lesson. This lets the field circuit be distinguished from the load path of the series-wound example; the connection name is not a description of armature shape.", ["A shunt-wound generator's field is connected in _____ with the load.", "parallel", ["shunt"]]);
add("compound", "field-connections", "generator", [55], "What does a compound-wound DC generator combine?", ["Series and shunt field arrangements", "Only two identical disconnected primary cells", "Only an AC slip ring and a hydrometer", "Only two battery separator materials"], "The lesson combines series and shunt field arrangements in the compound machine. The name describes the field connection system rather than a second name for a lap or wave armature winding.", ["The generator combining series and shunt fields is _____ wound.", "compound"]);
add("rheostat", "field-connections", "generator", [53, 54], "Which variable-resistance device is named for generator output/terminal-voltage control in these field circuits?", ["Rheostat", "Hydrometer", "Salt bridge", "Fixed separator"], "The lesson uses a rheostat in the field-control discussion. Changing the field circuit changes magnetic excitation and therefore generated output; it is not a mechanical change to the armature's coil count during operation.", ["The variable-resistance device named for field control is a _____.", "rheostat"]);
add("maintenance-mount", "maintenance", "generator", [58, 59], "Why is secure generator mounting emphasized?", ["The generator installation is subject to substantial vibration", "Mounting bolts determine the battery electrolyte chemistry", "Loose mounting improves commutator contact", "Vibration replaces the need for a drive connection"], "The maintenance slides identify mounting as a high-vibration concern. The generator must be secured using the applicable instructions rather than treating attachment condition as unrelated to reliable operation.", ["The generator mounting is a high-_____ location in the lesson.", "vibration"]);
add("maintenance-connections", "maintenance", "generator", [60], "What condition is specified for generator electrical connections?", ["Clean and correctly torqued", "Oily and deliberately loose", "Tightened only by appearance without data", "Disconnected while expected to deliver normal output"], "The source pairs connection cleanliness with correct torque. Contact condition and mechanical security affect reliable current paths; actual torque values come from the specific equipment instructions.", ["Generator electrical connections must be clean and correctly _____.", "torqued"]);
add("maintenance-clean", "maintenance", "generator", [61], "What contamination does the generator-maintenance slide call out for removal?", ["Dirt and oil", "All specified electrical insulation", "All brush material regardless of condition", "All magnetic laminations"], "Dirt and oil are contamination to be cleaned using the correct maintenance method. Removing insulation, laminations or serviceable brush material would not be the intended interpretation of cleaning.", ["The maintenance slide identifies dirt and _____ as contamination to clean.", "oil"]);
add("maintenance-brush", "maintenance", "generator", [62], "What brush condition does the lesson say may need correcting?", ["An unseated brush", "A normally insulated brush holder", "A correctly seated contact surface", "A correctly installed field frame"], "The source tells the technician to check brush condition and notes that brushes can become unseated. Reseating is a specific maintenance action controlled by the generator's instructions, not a reason to adjust every good brush.", ["A brush with poor seating may need to be re-_____.", "seated"]);
