add("regulator-types", "regulation", "generator", [66], "Which two regulator types does the lesson list?", ["Electromechanical and solid-state", "Only primary and secondary", "Only ring and drum", "Only acid and alkaline"], "The voltage-control slide distinguishes electromechanical devices from solid-state devices. The other pairs classify cells, armatures or chemistry and do not name the listed regulator technologies.", ["One listed regulator technology is solid-_____.", "state"]);
add("protection-overvoltage", "regulation", "generator", [68], "Which protective function addresses generator voltage becoming excessive?", ["Overvoltage protection", "Battery electrolyte-density compensation only", "Brush seating alone", "A simple load-off hydrometer reading"], "Overvoltage protection is separately listed alongside normal voltage regulation. The protective function addresses excessive output rather than treating every output excursion as an acceptable consequence of engine-speed change.", ["Protection against excessive output voltage is _____ protection.", "overvoltage"]);
add("protection-parallel", "regulation", "generator", [68], "Which operating arrangement is explicitly included among generator-control functions?", ["Parallel generator operation", "Connecting all battery chemistries to a common spill tray", "Replacing all field circuits with a permanent short", "Operating without any required output range"], "The control-function list includes parallel operation, in addition to regulation and protection. This means coordinated operation of generators, not indiscriminate joining of unrelated electrical or chemical systems.", ["The lesson lists _____ generator operation as a control-system function.", "parallel"]);
add("protection-excitation", "regulation", "generator", [68], "Which listed function addresses excessive field excitation?", ["Overexcitation protection", "Specific-gravity correction", "A carbon-zinc salt bridge", "A mechanical battery cap"], "The lesson explicitly lists overexcitation protection. Excitation belongs to the magnetic field circuit, so this is a different concern from battery electrolyte density or case servicing.", ["Protection against too much field excitation is _____ protection.", "overexcitation"]);
add("differential-voltage", "regulation", "generator", [68], "Which voltage-comparison function is named in the lesson's generator-control list?", ["Differential voltage", "Electrolyte-specific gravity", "Peak mechanical mounting torque", "Battery plate sediment volume"], "Differential voltage appears in the course list with voltage regulation, parallel operation and protective sensing. The question identifies the named electrical control function rather than inferring an unprovided aircraft-specific setting.", ["The voltage-comparison function in the list is _____ voltage.", "differential"]);
add("starter-generator", "regulation", "generator", [69, 70, 71], "What dual-purpose electrical machine is identified for some small turbine-powered aircraft?", ["DC starter-generator", "Only a hydrometer-operated motor", "A primary carbon-zinc charging cell", "A static split-ring with no windings"], "The source identifies a machine with starter and generator functions, using series and parallel field arrangements under GCU control. The same machine's function changes with its electrical configuration and operating mode.", ["The dual-purpose machine is a DC starter-_____.", "generator"]);
add("starter-field-control", "regulation", "generator", [70, 71], "What controls switching of the starter-generator's field arrangements in the lesson?", ["GCU", "Hydrometer", "Battery separator", "Only the commutator's mica insulation"], "The lesson assigns series/parallel field switching and protective control to the GCU. The generator's passive insulation and the battery's servicing instruments do not perform that switching function.", ["The starter-generator field switching is controlled by the _____ (abbreviation).", "GCU"]);
add("zener", "regulation", "generator", [71], "Which semiconductor device is explicitly named in the lesson's starter-generator control discussion?", ["Zener diode", "Light-emitting diode", "Phototransistor", "Silicon-controlled rectifier"], "The slide names a Zener diode alongside solenoids and relays in the control arrangement. These are different device categories: the diode is the named semiconductor, while the other two are electromagnetic devices. The slide does not establish a universal aircraft trip-voltage setting.", ["The semiconductor named in the GCU slide is a _____ diode.", "Zener"]);
